course: dictionary
lesson: 4059
title: Word from the stories: genie
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A genie is a magic spirit from old stories of Arabia and the Middle East. Often it lives in a lamp or a bottle, and whoever sets it free must be obeyed. Readers find genies in tales like «Aladdin», so the story's setting gives you a clue.
explain: Context helps you here. In a story about a Djinn (another word for a genie), the text says he travels «rolling in a cloud of dust» and calls the Camel «My long and bubbling friend». Those clues show a big, dramatic, slightly magical character who is joking with the Camel.
explain: The name «Bubbles» is a clue too. The text says camels grumble and bubble, so the Djinn is teasing the Camel with a nickname that fits him. Always ask what a word would mean in this sentence, then check your meaning in the sentence.
fact: A genie is a magic spirit from Arabian stories, often living in a lamp or bottle.
fact: A genie must obey whoever sets it free, and may grant wishes.
fact: The older Arabic word for a genie is «jinn» or «djinn».
fact: The informal phrase «the genie is out of the bottle» means something has happened that cannot be undone.
fact: In Kipling's «How the Camel Got His Hump», the Djinn in charge of All Deserts gives the lazy Camel a hump.
exercise: What is a genie? | A lamp made of brass | A type of camel | *A magic spirit from Arabian stories | A desert storm
exercise: «The genie is out of the bottle» means what? | The bottle is empty and needs filling | *Something has happened that cannot be undone | Someone is very thirsty | A genie has been found and is hiding
exercise: In stories, what must a genie do for whoever sets it free? | *Obey them, and it may grant wishes | Turn them into a camel | Hide in the sand | Sing in the desert
test: Why does the Djinn call the Camel «Bubbles»? | Because bubbles are his favourite food | Because the Camel can fly | *Because camels grumble and bubble | Because the Camel lives in a bottle
test: Which older Arabic word is the origin of genie? | wish | camel | lamp | *jinn
exam: What do the words «rolling in a cloud of dust» suggest about the Djinn's arrival? | He is tiny and quiet | *He arrives in a dramatic, dusty, showy way | He is wet from the rain | He is sleeping under a blanket
